              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
          %h2#double_copy_and_classical_field_theory Double copy and classical field theory
          %h3#double_copy_and_kerrschild_metric Double copy and Kerr-Schild metric
          %h4#definitions Definitions
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerr-Schild+spacetime"} Kerr-Schild metric
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation"} perturbation
                of a flat
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+metric"} Minkowski metric
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{\mu\nu}
                of the form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                      %mo +
                      %mi κ
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi ν
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{\mu\nu} + \kappa \phi k_{\mu}k_{\nu}
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi κ
                      %mo =
                      %msqrt
                        %mrow
                          %mn 32
                          %mi π
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa=\sqrt{32\pi G_{\mathrm{N}}}
                is a constant with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{\mathrm{N}}
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Newton%27s+constant"} Newton's constant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_\mu
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} null
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covector"} covector
                satisfying the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesic"} geodesic
                property, i.e.
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi ν
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi ν
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{\mu\nu}k^\mu k^\nu = g_{\mu\nu}k^\mu k^\nu =0, \quad (k\cdot\partial)k^\mu =0.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em single copy
                gauge field (
                %a{:href => "#MOW15"}> MOW 15
                ) of this gravitational field is defined for any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                by
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi μ
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\mu} = (c^a \mathbf{T}_a) \phi k_\mu
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                        %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^a\mathbf{T}_a \in \mathfrak{g}
                is an arbitrary constant
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color charge
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector"} vector
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^a
                in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} basis
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mathbf{T}_a\}
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝔤
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %li
              %p
                Conversely, if we start from a gauge field of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi A
                        %mi μ
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ϕ
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_{\mu} = (c^a \mathbf{T}_a) \phi k_\mu
                for any constant
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color charge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                        %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝔤
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c^a\mathbf{T}_a \in \mathfrak{g}
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} null
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covector"} covector
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_\mu
                satisfying the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesic"} geodesic
                property, we can define its
                %em double copy
                gravitational field by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kerr-Schild+spacetime"} Kerr-Schild metric
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mrow
                            %mi μ
                            %mi ν
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mrow
                            %mi μ
                            %mi ν
                        %mo +
                        %mi κ
                        %mi ϕ
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi μ
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi ν
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\mu\nu} + \kappa \phi k_{\mu}k_{\nu}
            %li
              %p
                Otherwise, if we repeat the procedure of replacing a covector
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k_\mu
                with any fixed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color charge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mi b
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∈
                      %mover
                        %mi 𝔤
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\tilde{c}^b \tilde{\mathbf{T}}_b)\in\tilde{\mathfrak{g}}
                we can get a
                %em zeroth copy
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi c
                        %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi T
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mover
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mi b
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi T
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                        %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi = (c^a \mathbf{T}_a)\otimes(\tilde{c}^b \tilde{\mathbf{T}}_b)\phi
              %p
                where the new
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{G}
                can be chosen different from the previous
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi G
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h4#field_equations Field equations
          %p
            By following (
            %a{:href => "#MOW15"}> MOW 15
            ) we have a comparison of the field equations. Assume without loss of generality that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi k
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^0=1
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The vacuum
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+equations"} Einstein equations
                for the metric
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mrow
                          %mi μ
                          %mi ν
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\mu\nu}
                are
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R=0
                (where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is the
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ricci+curvature"} Ricci curvature
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msubsup
                                %mi R
                                %mrow
                                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                  %mn 0
                                %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mfrac
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 2
                              %msup
                                %mo ∇
                                %mn 2
                              %mi ϕ
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msubsup
                                %mi R
                                %mrow
                                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                  %mn 0
                                %mi i
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mfrac
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 2
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi j
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %msup
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo −
                                %msup
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo )
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msubsup
                                %mi R
                                %mrow
                                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                                  %mi j
                                %mi i
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mfrac
                                %mn 1
                                %mn 2
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi l
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %msup
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi l
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo +
                                %msub
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi l
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo −
                                %msup
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi l
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi i
                                %msub
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo )
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{aligned}
                      R^0_{\;0} &amp;= \frac{1}{2}\nabla^2\phi &amp;&amp; = 0 \\
                      R^i_{\;0} &amp;= \frac{1}{2}\partial_j \left(\partial^j(\phi k^i)-\partial^i(\phi k^j)\right) &amp;&amp; =0 \\
                      R^i_{\;j} &amp;= \frac{1}{2}\partial_l \left(\partial^i(\phi k^l k^j)+\partial_j(\phi k^l k^i)-\partial^l(\phi k^i k_j)\right) &amp;&amp; =0
                      \\end{aligned}
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxwell+equations"} Maxwell equations
                for the gauge field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                are
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mi F
                        %mo =
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{d} F = 0
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                              %mi F
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mn 0
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msup
                                %mo ∇
                                %mn 2
                              %mi ϕ
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                              %mi F
                              %msup
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mi i
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %msub
                                %mo ∂
                                %mi j
                              %mrow
                                %mo (
                                %msup
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo −
                                %msup
                                  %mo ∂
                                  %mi i
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ϕ
                                %msup
                                  %mi k
                                  %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                %mo )
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo =
                              %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\begin{aligned}
                      (\mathrm{d}F)^0 &amp;= \nabla^2\phi &amp;&amp; = 0 \\
                      (\mathrm{d}F)^i &amp;= \partial_j \left(\partial^j(\phi k^i)-\partial^i(\phi k^j)\right) &amp;&amp; = 0
                      \\end{aligned}
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
                for the scalar field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
                are
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ∇
                        %mn 2
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla^2\Phi = 0
          %h4#outlook Outlook
          %p Summarizing, we have the following table:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th zeroth copy
                %th single copy
                %th double copy
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi Φ
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi c
                          %mi a
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi T
                          %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mover
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi b
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi T
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\Phi = (c^a \mathbf{T}_a)\otimes(\tilde{c}^b \tilde{\mathbf{T}}_b)\phi\;
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi μ
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi c
                          %mi a
                        %msub
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi T
                          %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi ϕ
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi μ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;A_{\mu} = (c^a \mathbf{T}_a) \phi k_\mu\;
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mrow
                            %mi μ
                            %mi ν
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mrow
                            %mi μ
                            %mi ν
                        %mo +
                        %mi κ
                        %mi ϕ
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi μ
                        %msub
                          %mi k
                          %mi ν
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{\mu\nu} + \kappa \phi k_{\mu}k_{\nu}\;
          %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/doublecopy.png", :width => "700px"}/
            %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
              Picture grabbed from (
              %a{:href => "#BSW20"}> BSW 20
              ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Examples of classical double copy of gauge fields:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                  solution
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                  solution
                %th ref.
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} electric monopole
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwarzschild+spacetime"} Schwarzschild spacetime
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (
                  %a{:href => "#MOW15"}> MOW 15
                  )
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} magnetic monopole
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  massless
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taub-NUT+spacetime"} Taub-NUT spacetime
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (
                  %a{:href => "#LMOW15"}> LMOW 15
                  ,
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#Kim24b"} Kim 24b
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} self-dual dyon
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  self-dual
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taub-NUT+space"} Taub-NUT space
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (
                  %a{:href => "#Kim24a"}> Kim 24a
                  )
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} planar wave
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pp-wave"} pp-wave
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (
                  %a{:href => "#MOW15"}> MOW 15
                  )
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} planar shockwave
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Aichelburg-Sexl shockwave
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  (
                  %a{:href => "#BSW20"}> BSW 20
                  )
          %h2#double_copy_and_topology Double copy and topology
          %p
            From (
            %a{:href => "#LMOW15"}> LMOW 15
            ) we know that in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/charges"} charges
            we have the following correspondence:
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
                  solution
                %th
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                  solution
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric+charge"} electric charge
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NUT+charge"} NUT charge
          %p
            The topological consequences were explored by (
            %a{:href => "#AWW20"}> AWW 20
            ):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopole"} magnetic monopole
                is geometrically a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
                of the form
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mn 1
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mtext worldline
                      %mo ×
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mn 3
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                            %mn 0
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mtext transverse space
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mo ≃
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} diff
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mn 1
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mtext worldline
                      %mo ×
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi ℝ
                              %mo +
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mtext radial dir.
                      %mo ×
                      %munder
                        %munder
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mn 2
                          %mo ⏟
                        %mtext angular dir.
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi B
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \underset{ \text{worldline} }\underbrace{\mathbb{R}^{1}} \times \underset{ \text{transverse space} }\underbrace{ (\mathbb{R}^3-\{0\}) } \;\,\simeq_{\mathrm{diff}}\;\,  \underset{ \text{worldline} }\underbrace{\mathbb{R}^{1}} \times \underset{ \text{radial dir.} }\underbrace{\mathbb{R}^+} \times \underset{ \text{angular dir.} }\underbrace{S^2} \longrightarrow B U(1)
              %p
                which is trivial only on the worldline
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1}
                of the monopole. Therefore, since we have the homotopy
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mn 3
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ≃
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^3-\{0\} \simeq S^2
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
                of the bundle will be an element
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ∈
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ≅
                        %mi ℤ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F] \in H^2(S^2,\mathbb{Z})\cong \mathbb{Z}
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mover
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Vol
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [F] = \tilde{g} [\mathrm{Vol}_{S^2}]
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Vol
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Vol}_{S^2}
                is the volume form of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{g}\in\mathbb{Z}
                is the quantized
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            %li
              %p
                The massless
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taub-NUT+spacetime"} Taub-NUT spacetime
                with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NUT+charge"} NUT charge
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle"} circle bundle
                too. In fact it is diffeomorphic to the manifold
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mo +
                        %mo ×
                        %mi L
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^+\times L(1,N)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ×
                      %mi L
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times L(1,N)
                is the
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 3
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo ≅
                        %msup
                          %mi H
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mn 2
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N\in\mathbb{Z}\cong H^2(S^2,\mathbb{Z})
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^1
                fiber has the interpretation of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time"} time
                direction, which is periodic and non-trivially fibrated on the sphere
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
                of the angular directions.
          %p
            Therefore the double copy procedure exchange the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+class"} first Chern class
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopole"} magnetic monopole
            with the one of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taub-NUT+spacetime"} Taub-NUT spacetime
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi N
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde{g} \mapsto N.
          %h2#double_copy_and_wilson_lines Double copy and Wilson lines
          %p
            The classical double copy of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
            was introduced by (
            %a{:href => "#AWW20"}> AWW 20
            ). We can use as gravitational
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
            on spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e^{i S_{\mathrm{kin}}}: [S^1, M]\rightarrow U(1)
            of a test particle. For any loop
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma\in[S^1, M]
            we can then write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} grav
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi i
                    %mi m
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi γ
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi s
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mrow
                            %mi μ
                            %mi ν
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mi μ
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                            %mi s
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mi ν
                          %mrow
                            %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                            %mi s
                        %mo )
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_{\mathrm{grav}}(\gamma) = e^{i S_{\mathrm{kin}}}(\gamma) = \exp \left(i m \int_\gamma \mathrm{d}s \left(g_{\mu\nu}\frac{\mathrm{d}x^\mu}{\mathrm{d}s}\frac{\mathrm{d}x^\nu}{\mathrm{d}s}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \right) \;\in\, U(1)
          %p
            If we assume that the metric is of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                    %mo +
                    %mi κ
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{\mu\nu} = \eta_{\mu\nu} + \kappa h_{\mu\nu}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} grav
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_{\mathrm{grav}}(\gamma)
            at first order in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            and obtain
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} grav
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi κ
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi γ
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi s
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mrow
                        %mi μ
                        %mi ν
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mi s
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi ν
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mi s
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_{\mathrm{grav}}(\gamma) = \exp \left(\frac{i \kappa}{2} \int_\gamma \mathrm{d}s\, h_{\mu\nu}\frac{\mathrm{d}x^\mu}{\mathrm{d}s}\frac{\mathrm{d}x^\nu}{\mathrm{d}s} \right) \;\in\, U(1)
          %p
            where the mass
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            is absorbed into the new parameter
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            of the
            %em single copy
            gauge field along the same path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi W
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} gauge
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi i
                    %mi g
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi γ
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                    %mi s
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msubsup
                      %mi A
                      %mi μ
                      %mi a
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %msup
                          %mi x
                          %mi μ
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                        %mi s
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi T
                      %mi a
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W_{\mathrm{gauge}}(\gamma) = \mathcal{P}\exp \left(i g \int_\gamma \mathrm{d}s\, A_{\mu}^a \frac{\mathrm{d}x^\mu}{\mathrm{d}s}\mathbf{T}_a \right) \;\in\, G
          %p
            Thus we immediately see that the double copy rules for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+line"} Wilson line
            are the following:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi T
                    %mi a
                  %mo ↦
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi ν
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "1em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi g
                  %mo ↦
                  %mfrac
                    %mi κ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{T}_a \mapsto  \frac{\mathrm{d}x^\nu}{\mathrm{d}s}, \quad\; g \mapsto  \frac{\kappa}{2}
          %p
            Notice that they precisely mirror the BCJ prescription of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+copy"} double copy
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            by exchanging color data with kinematic data and gauge coupling constant with its gravitational analogue.
          %p
            This suggests that this formulation can be a bridge to formally connect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+double+copy"} classical double copy
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+copy"} double copy
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
          %h2#double_copy_and_sduality Double copy and S-duality
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#ABSP19"}> ABSP 19
            ) it was proved that an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electric-magnetic+duality"} electric-magnetic duality
            (i.e.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ehlers+transformation"} Ehlers transformation
            on the double copy gravitational field. In other words the following ideal diagram commutes:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                            %mi electric
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi monopole
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi double
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi copy
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                            %mi Schwarzschild
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi black
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi hole
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo
                            %mrow
                              %mi S
                              %mo −
                              %mi duality
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mrow
                              %mi Ehlers
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi transformation
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                            %mi magnetic
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi monopole
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi double
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi copy
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                            %mi NUT
                            %mo −
                            %mi charged
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mi spacetime
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{{electric \; monopole} &amp; \overset{{\;\; double \; copy \;\;}}{\to} &amp; {Schwarzschild \; black \; hole}\\
                  \&amp; \\
                  ^{{S-duality}}\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{{Ehlers \; transformation}}\\
                  \&amp; \\
                  {magnetic \; monopole}&amp; \underset{{\;\; double \; copy \;\;}}{\to} &amp; {NUT-charged \; spacetime}}
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KLT+relations"} KLT relations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwarzschild+spacetime"}> Schwarzschild spacetime
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taub-NUT+space"} Taub-NUT space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+physics"} duality in physics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory+results+applied+elsewhere"}> string theory results applied elsewhere
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open%2Fclosed+string+duality"} open/closed string duality
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magic+pyramid"}> magic pyramid
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magic+supergravity"} magic supergravity
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"}> Kaluza-Klein mechanism
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+monopole"} Kaluza-Klein monopole
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+QFT"} effective QFT
                incarnations of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open%2Fclosed+string+duality"} open/closed string duality
              %p
                relating (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"}> super
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
                to (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"}> super
                \-)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
                \:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KLT+relations"} KLT relations
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BCJ+relations"} BCJ relations
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+double+copy"} classical double copy
          %h2#references References
          %p Fundamental bibliography:
          %ul
            %li#MOW15
              %p
                Ricardo Monteiro, Donal O’Connell, Chris D. White,
                %em Black holes and the double copy
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.0239"}> arXiv:1410.0239
                )
            %li#LMOW15
              %p
                Andrés Luna, Ricardo Monteiro, Donal O’Connell, Chris D. White,
                %em The classical double copy for Taub-NUT spacetime
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1507.01869"}> arXiv:1507.01869
                )
            %li
              %p
                Chris D. White,
                %em The double copy: gravity from gluons
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.07056"}> arXiv:1708.07056
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Berman"}> David Berman
                , Erick Chacón, Andrés Luna, Chris D. White,
                %em The self-dual classical double copy, and the Eguchi-Hanson instanton
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.04063"}> arXiv:1809.04063
                )
            %li
              %p
                Kwangeon Kim, Kanghoon Lee, Ricardo Monteiro, Isobel Nicholson, David Peinador Veiga,
                %em The Classical Double Copy of a Point Charge
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02177"}> arXiv:1912.02177
                )
            %li#BSW20
              %p
                Nadia Bahjat-Abbas, Ricardo Stark-Muchão, Chris D. White,
                %em Monopoles, shockwaves and the classical double copy
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.09918"}> arXiv:2001.09918
                )
            %li#Kim24a
              %p
                Joon-Hwi Kim,
                %em Single Kerr-Schild Metric for Taub-NUT Instanton
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.09518"}> arXiv:2405.09518
                )
            %li#Kim24b
              %p
                Joon-Hwi Kim,
                %em Newman-Janis Algorithm from Taub-NUT Instantons
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.19611"}> arXiv:2412.19611
                )
          %p Foundational issues:
          %ul
            %li
              Chris D. White,
              %em A Twistorial Foundation for the Classical Double Copy
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.02479"}> arXiv:2012.02479
              )
          %p
            Some global aspects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+double+copy"} classical double copy
            were explored in the following paper:
          %ul
            %li#AWW20
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luigi+Alfonsi"}> Luigi Alfonsi
              , Chris D. White, Sam Wikeley,
              %em Topology and Wilson lines: global aspects of the double copy
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07181"}> arXiv:2004.07181
              )
          %p
            In the following paper it is shown that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            on a gauge field corresponds to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ehlers+transformation"} Ehlers transformation
            on its double copy:
          %ul
            %li#ABSP19
              Rashid Alawadhi,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Berman"} David Berman
              %em S-duality and the Double Copy
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.06797"}> arXiv:1911.06797
              )
          %p
            The following paper is a proposal of extension of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+double+copy"} classical double copy
            to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+field+theory"} double field theory
          %ul
            %li
              Kanghoon Lee,
              %em Kerr-Schild Double Field Theory and Classical Double Copy
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.08443"}> arXiv:1807.08443
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Andres Luna, Silvia Nagy, Chris White,
              %em The convolutional double copy: a case study with a point
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.11254"}> arXiv:2004.11254
              )
          %p
            Discussion for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D11+supergravity"} D=11 supergravity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Berman"}> David Berman
              , Kwangeon Kim, Kanghoon Lee,
              %em The Classical Double Copy for M-theory from a Kerr-Schild Ansatz for Exceptional Field Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.08255"}> arXiv:2010.08255
              )
          %p
            Description via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity+algebras"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                \-algebras
          %ul
            %li
